From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Cui <bh@bugs.cc>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 03:44:41 -0400
Subject: feat: share one conversation history across accounts by default

`cswap run` gives every account a profile of its own under
`CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, and the profile's `projects/` (what `claude --resume`
lists) is per account unless history sharing is on. Upstream's
`run.shareHistory` setting (realiti4/claude-swap#439) makes the choice
durable, but it defaults to off, so forgetting it once means
`_sync_sharing` unlinks the profile from `~/.claude` and the account
starts a second, private history. The same-account fast path makes it
worse. When account N is the default login, `cswap run N` runs plain
`claude` on `~/.claude` and skips the profile entirely, so one account's
sessions land in two places depending on who was the default at the
time. Whoever switches the default and then reaches for `--resume` finds
a conversation missing (realiti4/claude-swap#239), and the profile of the
account that is the default can never be merged, because the code that
merges never runs for it.

A conversation belongs to the user, not to the account that happened to
run it, so on top of upstream's setting:

- `run.shareHistory` defaults to true. `--share-history` /
  `--no-share-history` still override it for one launch. On Windows,
  where history sharing is not available, the default falls back to
  per-account history silently; upstream's warning stays for a value the
  user set, and an explicit `--share-history` is still refused.
- `session.shareHistory`, the key earlier aswap releases stored this
  choice under, is honored while `run.shareHistory` is absent, so nobody's
  choice flips on upgrade.
- The same-account fast path syncs the profile before exec'ing plain
  claude, so history the account accumulated while it was not the default
  is merged into `~/.claude` and linked right away, instead of waiting for
  a launch that never comes while the account stays the default.

Together with the previous patch, every launch (`claude`, `cswap run N`
as the default or not) reads and writes one history, and a profile that
forked before the setting existed folds back in on its next launch
without losing a file.

